CLOSURE_CHECKS_CLASSIFIER_AUDIT: {
  "schema_version": 1,
  "migration_run_id": "17f884f4-9cd3-424e-ab5c-cc1157efaa48",
  "snapshot_timestamp": "2026-05-21T16:03:13Z",
  "ticket_id": "2fa0-a2d9-35b7-4101",
  "items": [
    {
      "original_text": "Application-level context-augmentation loop active for standard, deep (3 specialists + arch synthesis), and overlay reviewers; each can request additional context during its turn by emitting a structured request block in its assistant message per a documented contract (`docs/contracts/ci-review-context-request.md`). The dispatcher parses request blocks **only from assistant-role messages**, never re-parsing user-role / diff content (defending against the failure mode where a PR diff or a docs change containing literal example request syntax would otherwise be re-interpreted as a request). The dispatcher executes parsed requests locally and appends the results as a new user message before issuing the next `litellm.completion(...)` call. The provider tool-use API is not used \u2014 every call's message history is provider-agnostic plain text. Light tier dispatched in single-shot mode (no augmentation loop) and remains diff-only. Parser handles failure modes including the model emitting both a well-formed request block and a final-findings block in the same assistant message: well-formed-request takes precedence and findings are deferred to a subsequent turn. The structured-request format and the request-precedence rule are documented in the contract so request-block parsing is testable independently of the LLM call. Verified by context-request logs in fixture-corpus runs evidencing standard / deep reviewers grounding findings in fetched data (each such finding cites a `file:line` resolved by a recorded request-response cycle), light reviewers producing findings from the diff alone (no requests in the log), and parser unit tests against well-formed and malformed assistant outputs (including outputs that contain literal request-syntax in user-role positions, which must NOT trigger execution).",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior and capabilities (context-augmentation loop active, dispatcher parsing rules, request precedence) that remain true after implementation"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "`read_files` and `grep` tool implementations enforce a security boundary: repo-root jail (absolute paths and `..` parent traversal rejected; **paths canonicalized via realpath and re-verified to resolve under repo root, defending against committed or runtime-created symlinks pointing to /etc, $HOME, or sibling worktrees**); per-file size cap (configurable; default 256 KB); per-tool-result max output bytes (configurable; default 64 KB) with an explicit truncation marker appended when the cap is hit and an instruction to the model not to assert absence based on truncated content; **per-grep-invocation wall-clock timeout (configurable; default 5s) and binary-file exclusion (or use ripgrep with `--max-filesize`) to prevent ReDoS-class regex patterns from pinning a CI core**. Verified by unit tests covering absolute-path rejection, parent-traversal rejection, **symlink-escape rejection (test fixtures with both committed symlinks and runtime-created symlinks pointing outside repo root)**, file-size-cap rejection, multi-pattern multi-path tool argument acceptance, **grep-timeout enforcement with adversarial regex (e.g., `(a+)+$` against a long line)**, and truncation-marker emission.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes durable security properties and behavioral guarantees of the system (jailing, symlink defense, timeouts, truncation) that persist after implementation, not a one-time completion task"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Anthropic prompt caching active with two `cache_control` breakpoints (after the system prompt; after the diff). OpenAI prefix caching applies automatically. The `cache_control` placement is derived from the rendered message structure at call time (not hardcoded by index), defending against agents that inject agent-specific instructions between system and diff. **A prefix-bytes hash invariant test asserts the cached prefix is byte-identical across turns within a run**, defending against silent invalidation from per-turn dynamic content (timestamps, turn counters, agent_id interpolation, randomized tip ordering, trailing whitespace from template rendering). A cache-hit assertion emits a warning to CI logs when `usage.cache_creation_input_tokens > 0` does not occur on the first Anthropic call per review run **AND `usage.cache_read_input_tokens` is also zero** (tolerating the legitimate first-call cache-warm case where a CI job is retried within the 5-minute Anthropic cache TTL); subsequent turns warn when `cache_read_input_tokens` does not increase. Verified by unit test asserting `cache_control` placement at the documented boundaries, prefix-bytes-hash invariant test across turns, and integration test asserting cache-creation on turn 1 and cache-read on turn 2 against a fixture review.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system properties and invariants that remain true after implementation (caching active, hash invariants held, assertions in place), not a one-time completion task"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Soft-cap nudge cascade: a per-tier configurable soft cap (**default 15 turns**) triggers a wrap-up nudge instructing the model to emit final `reviewer-findings` JSON. **If the model continues emitting structured context-request blocks past `soft_cap + 3` turns, a second harder nudge runs.** **Post-nudge state-machine rule: when an assistant turn after the first nudge contains both a well-formed request block and a final-findings block, the request is ignored AND counts as a strike toward the second nudge \u2014 preventing infinite loops where deferring findings indefinitely defeats the cap.** If the model still does not emit final JSON after the second nudge, the review records as failed (fail-closed) via the existing `record-review.sh` integration. Verified by tests covering: nudge -> final JSON path; second nudge -> fail-closed recording; per-tier soft-cap override; post-nudge mixed request+findings turn -> request ignored, strike counted.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior (nudge cascade mechanism with configurable caps and state-machine rules) that will persist after implementation, not a one-time migration task"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Before / after fixture-corpus delta: post-loop CI runs surface a documented number of additional grounded findings (each citing a `file:line` resolved by a context request) compared to the pre-loop baseline captured in story 1 of epic c6de-76e7-5e4b-4968. Comparison runs at epic close as a one-shot script and the delta is reported in the close artifact; per-fixture grounded-finding counts shift from zero (pre) to non-zero (post) on at least 60% of fixtures. **Quality-not-just-presence assertion: on a curated subset (>=5 fixtures) where local /dso:review findings are known, post-change CI findings overlap with local findings on >=N critical/important items (N defined per fixture in the curated set), OR speculation-marker frequency (\"likely\", \"probably\", \"may break callers\") in CI finding text decreases by >=25% relative to the pre-loop baseline.**",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "transitional",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a one-time comparison measurement and validation task performed at epic close, not an ongoing system property"
      },
      "user_decision": "accept",
      "post_migration_section": "CC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Per-feature unit tests using `respx` / `httpx.MockTransport`-style provider mocks cover: structured-request parsing (well-formed + malformed assistant outputs, including outputs with literal request-syntax in user-role positions), context-request dispatch (single + multiple requests in one assistant turn), soft-cap nudge state machine (each branch of the cascade including post-nudge mixed turn rule), `cache_control` breakpoint placement under all four (tier x tools-present) combinations, prefix-bytes-hash invariant across turns, jail enforcement (including symlink escape rejection and grep-timeout enforcement), and output truncation. Integration tests exercise the loop end-to-end against both Anthropic and OpenAI providers with fixture reviews. (Provider/tier fallback dispatch tests live in c6de's SC6 \u2014 out of scope here.)",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable test coverage properties that will remain true after implementation, phrased as what the system's test suite encompasses"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    }
  ]
}
